---
import Page from '../../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { DEMO_URL, DOCKER_HUB_URL } from '../../../site';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Do students need an account to play?',
    a: '<p>No, in local mode: students type the PIN and a nickname, and only the teacher holds the host seat. In OIDC mode students sign in with their school account, unless the operator allows open access; the teacher then picks <b>Open access</b> at launch and the PIN and a nickname are enough. See <a href="/docs/host/invite-players/">Invite players</a>.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can QuizDock run in a classroom without Internet?',
    a: '<p>Yes. Answer <b>No</b> to “Does this instance reach the Internet?” in the setup wizard: the update check and the links to online media libraries are turned off. In local mode, with no web images in the quizzes, the server then makes no outgoing request. See <a href="/docs/operator/air-gapped/">Air-gapped</a>.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I bring my Kahoot quizzes?',
    a: '<p>Yes, as a spreadsheet made with Kahoot’s official template (.xlsx): wording, answer order, correct answers and time limits come across; pictures do not. A link to a Kahoot quiz cannot be imported. See <a href="/docs/host/import-a-quiz/">Import a quiz</a>.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can several teachers use the same instance?',
    a: '<p>Yes, one at a time in local mode: the instance has one host seat, taken by name. For several teachers presenting at the same moment, run QuizDock in OIDC mode and give each teacher the Host role. See <a href="/docs/host/getting-started/">Getting started</a>.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does QuizDock make a school compliant with data-protection law?',
    a: '<p>QuizDock makes no legal claim. Whoever runs the instance is the data controller. QuizDock gives choices: whether results are kept per student, whether every answer is recorded, and a notice on each phone saying which. See <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>.</p>',
  },
];
---

<Page
  title="QuizDock for education"
  description="A quiz in class on the projector, students joining by PIN or QR code without accounts, results kept on the school's own server or laptop."
  faq={faq}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <h1>A quiz in class, on the projector, without student accounts</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Ten questions at the end of a lesson, the class answering on their phones, nothing for the students to
      sign up for. QuizDock runs on the school’s server or on your own laptop: the quiz goes on the projector,
      students join with a PIN or a QR code, and the results stay in its database.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How fast can I get a quiz on the projector?</h2>
      <p>
        In local mode, open the <b>Host area</b>, type your name and choose <b>Take the host seat</b>. In
        <b>My quizzes</b>, pick a quiz marked ready and choose <b>Present</b>. The lobby shows a 6-digit PIN and a
        QR code; <b>Projection window</b> opens the big screen for the projector, and <b>Fullscreen</b> fills it.
        <b>Start the quiz</b> as soon as the class is in.
      </p>
      <p>
        Students open the address on the projection or scan the QR code, type a nickname of 2 to 20 characters,
        pick an avatar and tap <b>I’m ready</b>. Nobody but you has an account. A phone that loses the
        connection comes back with its score on the same device.
      </p>
      <p>
        The host seat is taken by name, with one host at a time: local mode is for a network you trust. See
        <a href="/plug-and-play/">Plug-and-play</a> and <a href="/docs/host/start-a-room/">Start a room</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure><img src="/screenshots/projection-lobby.png" alt="The projection lobby, with the join address, the PIN and a QR code" /><figcaption>What the projector shows while the class joins.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="/screenshots/player-play.png" alt="A student's phone during a question, one coloured tile with a shape per answer" /><figcaption>A student’s phone: colour and shape on every answer.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Can I use pictures, video and sound?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. Each question takes one visual (an image or an uploaded video) and a sound, and slides can sit
        between questions. Files are converted in your browser before upload; use Chrome or Edge for video,
        as Firefox cannot re-encode it. <b>Start the timer when the media ends</b> makes the class listen
        first, then answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        By default the projector plays the video and the sound, and phones in the room show the question’s
        image and stay silent. The media picker lists free libraries under <b>Find elsewhere:</b>, and each file carries its
        credit line. See <a href="/media/">Media</a> and <a href="/docs/host/media/">the media guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Do I have to retype the quizzes I already have?</h2>
      <p>
        No. <b>Import</b> in <b>My quizzes</b> takes a spreadsheet made with Kahoot’s official template: the
        wording, the answer order, the correct answers and the time limits come across, and the report says
        how many questions were imported. Pictures and sounds do not; incomplete rows arrive marked
        <b>Unfinished</b>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a PDF, a worksheet or a photo of a paper quiz, the Documentation gives a prompt and a format guide
        to hand a chatbot, which writes a file QuizDock imports. What you send goes to the chatbot’s provider,
        and its correct answers need checking. Colleagues on the same instance can also share quizzes through
        <b>Shared templates</b>. See <a href="/docs/host/import-a-quiz/">Import a quiz</a> and
        <a href="/docs/host/templates-and-community/">Templates and community</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Which questions did the class miss?</h2>
      <p>
        When you close the room, <b>Archive this quiz’s results</b> is ticked by default. The quiz’s
        <b>History</b> then lists each session; a session shows <b>Results by question</b> (answers, success
        rate, average time) and the <b>Players</b> table (rank, nickname, score, correct answers, streak).
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Export (CSV)</b> downloads that table as a file a spreadsheet opens. Each student’s answers, question
        by question, are kept only if <b>Record all answers</b> was on for that session. See
        <a href="/docs/host/reports-and-history/">Reports and history</a> and
        <a href="/docs/host/export-results/">Export results</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Will it work on the school network, or with no Internet?</h2>
      <p>
        Phones open the <b>Invitation address</b> chosen in the lobby: a detected network address of the
        machine, the public address, or <b>Other address…</b>. “localhost” only works on the computer itself,
        and the console warns about it. Docker Desktop hides the computer’s address: type it in, or set
        <code>HOST_LAN_IPS</code>. Many guest Wi-Fi networks keep devices from seeing each other; the setup
        wizard’s <b>Test from a phone</b> shows whether an address answers.
      </p>
      <p>
        With no Internet, answer <b>No</b> to “Does this instance reach the Internet?” in the wizard. See
        <a href="/docs/operator/networking/">Networking</a> and <a href="/self-hosted/">Self-hosted</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What is kept about my students?</h2>
      <p>
        You decide under <b>Before starting</b>, and the choice is fixed once the quiz starts.
        <b>Personalised tracking</b> is on by default; off, no individual result is kept, only the group’s
        overall results. <b>Record all answers</b> is off by default; turning it on asks for confirmation and
        warns that it is personal data.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each phone shows the matching notice, for instance “Your individual results are not recorded — only the
        group’s overall results are.” Archived sessions are kept 365 days and deleted when the operator runs the
        purge command; no archive is deleted automatically. The pages carry no analytics and set no tracking
        cookie. The update check, on by default, contacts GitHub; the offline answer turns it off. See
        <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> and <a href="/docs/admin/privacy-and-retention/">Privacy and retention</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="fine">How the server times every question and answer: <a href="/real-time/">Real time</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="next">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={DEMO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try the live demo ↗</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={DOCKER_HUB_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get it on Docker Hub ↗</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/host/getting-started/">Getting started as a host</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
